{#
  search-index.njk — the metadata the nav search box scores against.

  A .js file rather than .json, and loaded with a <script src> rather than
  fetch(), because fetch() on a file:// page is blocked by CORS in Chrome. The
  whole site is built to run off a USB stick; an index the stick can't read
  would be a search box that works only on the demo.

  Nothing here goes through the `rel` filter, which is the one place on the
  site that is true. Every other asset is referenced FROM a page and can be
  resolved against it; this one is a single file whose contents are read BY
  pages at every depth at once. It therefore stores root-relative URLs, and
  javascript/search.js prefixes them with the data-root the page hands it.

  One assignment and nothing else — no "ready" event to announce itself. The
  script tag's own load event already fires after this file has executed, so
  search.js can simply wait on that and know the array is there.

  `dump` is Nunjucks' JSON.stringify — it supplies the quotes and escapes the
  strings properly. escape_attr would be wrong here for the same reason it is
  wrong in webmanifest.njk: this is JavaScript, not HTML.
#}
window.__SEARCH_INDEX__ = {{ library.allEntries | searchIndex | dump | safe }};
